\taxon{Remark}
\title{Probabilistic interface versus implementation}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{language-model}

\p{Two implementations can determine the same next-token law while using
different tensor layouts or kernels. Conversely, changing a tokenizer or a
termination convention changes the domain on which the law is interpreted.
The model interface therefore includes the vocabulary and tokenizer as well
as the parameterized probability map.}

\p{Later FTIP comparisons will use the probability law to state semantic
claims. They will name architecture and runtime choices separately when those
choices affect training or inference cost. This prevents an implementation
speedup from being mistaken for a change in the represented distribution.}
